1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,546
"Learn the product, build relationships, ship a quick win" isn't wrong.

2
00:00:04,546 --> 00:00:09,451
It's just not specific to anything, which means it says nothing an interviewer or a

3
00:00:09,451 --> 00:00:13,233
new manager couldn't have guessed before you walked in the door.

4
00:00:13,233 --> 00:00:17,428
You've spent this whole course building things more specific than that.

5
00:00:17,428 --> 00:00:22,097
This lecture swaps the three generic milestones for three artifacts this course

6
00:00:22,097 --> 00:00:26,825
already taught you to build, timed to when they're actually achievable in a role

7
00:00:26,825 --> 00:00:28,066
touching AI features.

8
00:00:28,066 --> 00:00:29,779
Nothing here is new material.

9
00:00:29,779 --> 00:00:32,970
It's a calendar wrapped around tools you already have.

10
00:00:32,970 --> 00:00:36,812
Notice, too, that the timing itself is deliberate, not arbitrary.

11
00:00:36,812 --> 00:00:41,598
Each milestone needs the standing, or the information, that only the previous one

12
00:00:41,598 --> 00:00:42,130
produces.

13
00:00:42,130 --> 00:00:46,917
Day one of a new role has neither, which is exactly why the first thirty days are

14
00:00:46,917 --> 00:00:50,640
about finding out what's true, not about shipping anything yet.

15
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:55,486
This is also the natural close of this whole section, not a new topic bolted on at

16
00:00:55,486 --> 00:00:55,958
the end.

17
00:00:55,958 --> 00:01:00,036
Every lecture since 10.1 has been a specific skill for doing the job.

18
00:01:00,036 --> 00:01:04,704
This one is where those skills get a calendar, because a skill you can name but

19
00:01:04,704 --> 00:01:08,605
never schedule tends to stay theoretical past the first busy week.

20
00:01:09,155 --> 00:01:14,383
Days one to thirty aren't about changing anything, they're about finding out what's

21
00:01:14,383 --> 00:01:19,234
actually true, because a new hire has information value long before they have

22
00:01:19,234 --> 00:01:20,242
standing to act.

23
00:01:20,242 --> 00:01:25,282
Classify every AI-touching feature you can find, shipped or planned, using 1.6's

24
00:01:25,282 --> 00:01:30,511
archetypes, and check honestly whether any of them has an eval or an 8.1-style risk

25
00:01:30,511 --> 00:01:31,834
register row already.

26
00:01:31,834 --> 00:01:35,047
Most new AI PMs find at least one that has neither.

27
00:01:35,047 --> 00:01:38,575
That's not a criticism of the team that came before you.

28
00:01:38,575 --> 00:01:43,363
It's the single most useful thing you can hand your new manager in week two.

29
00:01:43,363 --> 00:01:47,835
Days thirty-one to sixty are where the audit turns into something real.

30
00:01:47,835 --> 00:01:52,812
Pick the single gap that matters most, small enough to actually finish inside a

31
00:01:52,812 --> 00:01:57,726
month, and fix it visibly: a 4.2-style golden set for the most fragile shipped

32
00:01:57,726 --> 00:02:02,766
feature, or the first honest row in an 8.1 register that didn't exist before you

33
00:02:02,766 --> 00:02:03,270
arrived.

34
00:02:03,270 --> 00:02:05,538
The point isn't the artifact's size.

35
00:02:05,538 --> 00:02:10,263
It's that it's finished and checkable, which a plan or a proposal never is.

36
00:02:10,813 --> 00:02:13,569
Days sixty-one to ninety are about durability.

37
00:02:13,569 --> 00:02:17,584
Could a teammate pick this up and continue it if you left tomorrow?

38
00:02:17,584 --> 00:02:22,617
That question is the real test of day ninety, more than whether the dashboard or the

39
00:02:22,617 --> 00:02:24,295
roadmap line looks finished.

40
00:02:24,845 --> 00:02:30,215
Notice the good column is smaller, not bigger, and looks less impressive in the room

41
00:02:30,215 --> 00:02:31,557
on day thirty itself.

42
00:02:31,557 --> 00:02:36,671
That trade is worth making anyway, because the bad column's comprehensiveness is

43
00:02:36,671 --> 00:02:38,972
exactly what makes it unfalsifiable.

44
00:02:38,972 --> 00:02:44,277
Nobody can check "here's everything I've learned" against anything, six weeks later

45
00:02:44,277 --> 00:02:44,789
or ever.

46
00:02:44,789 --> 00:02:50,030
This is 10.2's calibration principle again, aimed at your own first update instead

47
00:02:50,030 --> 00:02:51,245
of a stakeholder's.

48
00:02:51,245 --> 00:02:56,486
A single named finding plus a dated commitment is a claim reality can prove wrong,

49
00:02:56,486 --> 00:03:01,792
which is exactly what makes it trustworthy in a way a comprehensive-sounding survey

50
00:03:01,792 --> 00:03:02,367
never is.

51
00:03:02,367 --> 00:03:05,627
There's a compounding effect worth naming here too.

52
00:03:05,627 --> 00:03:10,805
A manager who watches one specific, dated commitment land on time starts trusting

53
00:03:10,805 --> 00:03:12,339
the next one by default.

54
00:03:12,339 --> 00:03:17,580
A manager who's only ever seen broad, unfalsifiable updates has no evidence either

55
00:03:17,580 --> 00:03:22,758
way, and has to keep re-evaluating you from scratch every single time, which is a

56
00:03:22,758 --> 00:03:26,657
slower way to earn the trust this whole plan is actually for.

57
00:03:27,207 --> 00:03:32,043
Say the honest caveat, because finishing a plan exactly as written, regardless of

58
00:03:32,043 --> 00:03:36,880
what the audit actually finds, is its own failure mode, not a sign of discipline.

59
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:41,656
This is 10.1's sprint-theatre point again, aimed at a whole quarter instead of a

60
00:03:41,656 --> 00:03:42,850
single feature idea.

61
00:03:42,850 --> 00:03:47,686
A ninety-day plan followed to the letter no matter what days one to thirty reveal

62
00:03:47,686 --> 00:03:51,567
stops being judgement and becomes a performance of having a plan.

63
00:03:51,567 --> 00:03:56,403
The entire reason to spend the first thirty days auditing is that the audit might

64
00:03:56,403 --> 00:03:58,613
change what the next sixty should be.

65
00:03:58,613 --> 00:04:03,628
If that happens, say so plainly, the same way 10.2 asked you to replace a hedge with

66
00:04:03,628 --> 00:04:04,464
a real number.

67
00:04:04,464 --> 00:04:09,419
"The plan changed because of what I found in week two, here's the new day sixty" is

68
00:04:09,419 --> 00:04:14,255
a stronger sentence to bring to your manager than quietly forcing the calendar to

69
00:04:14,255 --> 00:04:17,121
match an assumption the audit already disproved.

70
00:04:17,671 --> 00:04:22,453
That's the standard worth writing toward now, before day one makes it urgent, and

71
00:04:22,453 --> 00:04:27,235
the exercise is worth doing for the real role in front of you, not a hypothetical

72
00:04:27,235 --> 00:04:27,471
one.

73
00:04:27,471 --> 00:04:32,017
Most people writing this for the first time realise they can already name the

74
00:04:32,017 --> 00:04:36,799
day-thirty finding, even before the job starts, just from what they've noticed in

75
00:04:36,799 --> 00:04:38,511
the interview process itself.

76
00:04:38,511 --> 00:04:40,282
That's worth writing down now.

77
00:04:40,282 --> 00:04:45,123
It's much easier to confirm a suspicion on day five than to form one from nothing.

78
00:04:45,123 --> 00:04:49,905
Treat day thirty itself as the plan's own checkpoint, not just a milestone inside

79
00:04:49,905 --> 00:04:50,083
it.

80
00:04:50,083 --> 00:04:55,042
Reread what you wrote today against what the audit actually found, and let the sixty

81
00:04:55,042 --> 00:04:57,580
and ninety day rows change if they need to.

82
00:04:57,580 --> 00:05:02,303
A plan that can't survive contact with its own first month of evidence was never

83
00:05:02,303 --> 00:05:05,196
really a plan, just a date with a guess attached.

84
00:05:05,746 --> 00:05:07,525
One thing before you move on.

85
00:05:07,525 --> 00:05:12,186
Write the plan using this lecture's table, for the role you actually have or

86
00:05:12,186 --> 00:05:13,352
actually want next.

87
00:05:13,352 --> 00:05:17,952
A generic template rewritten in your own words is still a generic template.

88
00:05:17,952 --> 00:05:23,104
Then name the day-sixty artifact specifically, not as a category but as a real thing

89
00:05:23,104 --> 00:05:28,011
with a real feature attached, so day thirty-one already knows what it's building

90
00:05:28,011 --> 00:05:28,441
toward.

91
00:05:28,441 --> 00:05:33,409
Next lecture is the capstone brief: ship an AI feature spec, the final project of

92
00:05:33,409 --> 00:05:38,377
this course, where every artifact you've built gets used on one feature, start to

93
00:05:38,377 --> 00:05:38,807
finish.
